\paragraph{Gradient evolution and intervention design.}
\label{sec:gradient_evolution_main}
Gradient decomposition (Table~\ref{tab:gradient_decomposition}, Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_analysis}) reveals that the composition of $\nabla_\theta e_{\Delta t}$ evolves substantially during training: at 55K steps (CIVO onset), KL dynamics contributes 50.7\%, observation decoders 25.8\%, and the reward head 23.5\%; by convergence (95K), observation decoders dominate at 92.7\% while the reward head diminishes to 0.0\%.  This explains why reward-only SGS (exp\_016e) is functionally insufficient---it targets a pathway whose contribution vanishes---while full-pathway SGS, which removes the dominant observation decoder gradient, \sgsclaim{} (peak NVE $\sgsnve{}\times$, eval \sgseval{}).  Transient threshold exceedances from the residual 7\% KL pathway indicate that observation decoder gradients are the primary but not sole CIVO driver.

